<section class="law-guides-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="law-guide-grid">
      <article class="law-guide-card"><span class="law-guide-number">01</span><h2>Family protection and RA 9262</h2><p>RA 9262, or the Anti-Violence Against Women and Their Children Act, addresses physical, sexual, psychological, and economic abuse in covered relationships.</p><p class="law-guide-note"><strong>Prepare:</strong> A timeline, messages, medical or police records, and information about immediate safety concerns.</p></article>
      <article class="law-guide-card"><span class="law-guide-number">02</span><h2>Contracts and obligations</h2><p>The Civil Code provides the basic framework for agreements, consent, obligations, breach, damages, and remedies. Clear terms and complete records are important before signing.</p><p class="law-guide-note"><strong>Prepare:</strong> The complete contract, amendments, receipts, messages, and the specific promise or deadline in question.</p></article>
      <article class="law-guide-card"><span class="law-guide-number">03</span><h2>Property and land concerns</h2><p>Ownership, boundaries, leases, deeds, titles, tax declarations, and sales often involve several documents and overlapping facts that should be reviewed together.</p><p class="law-guide-note"><strong>Prepare:</strong> Copies of the title, tax declaration, deed or lease, survey documents, receipts, and a short history of the property.</p></article>
      <article class="law-guide-card"><span class="law-guide-number">04</span><h2>Succession and estates</h2><p>Inheritance may involve a will, compulsory heirs, estate settlement, partition, property records, and taxes. The family relationship and ownership history can change the process.</p><p class="law-guide-note"><strong>Prepare:</strong> Death certificate, birth or marriage records, will if available, asset list, titles, and known debts.</p></article>
      <article class="law-guide-card"><span class="law-guide-number">05</span><h2>Labor and employment</h2><p>Employment concerns may involve wages, benefits, leave, disciplinary action, termination, workplace policies, or independent-contractor arrangements under the Labor Code and related rules.</p><p class="law-guide-note"><strong>Prepare:</strong> Contract, payslips, notices, company policies, attendance records, and a dated account of what happened.</p></article>
      <article class="law-guide-card"><span class="law-guide-number">06</span><h2>Criminal process and due process</h2><p>A complaint, investigation, subpoena, arrest, or court case has different stages and deadlines. A person involved should understand the document received and avoid ignoring formal notices.</p><p class="law-guide-note"><strong>Prepare:</strong> The complaint or notice, dates, identification, supporting records, witness details, and any prior communication from authorities.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
